---
/*
 * Start — the three lines, and what is true about each of them today.
 *
 * ================== READ THIS BEFORE EDITING THE HEADING ==================
 * The heading and the commands are ONE decision, not two. docs/marketing/SITE-BRIEF.md
 * records why, and it is the kind of coupling that gets "tidied" apart:
 *
 *   friendly heading + flag-carrying command  ->  a broken promise: the flags say a stranger
 *                                                 needs a project URL they have not got
 *   grim heading     + bare command           ->  needlessly grim, and wrong about the code
 *   heading and command agreeing              ->  honest, which is the only stable state
 *
 * So HEADING and STEPS live in the same constant below and move together.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE GATE HAS FLIPPED, AND BOTH LINES MOVED AT ONCE. 2026-07-27.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The superseded pair, kept dead so nobody re-derives it from git history:
 *   ~~HEADING = "What you run once you're invited."~~
 *   ~~step 3  = cswarm working-on "..." --url https://<ref>.supabase.co --anon-key <key>~~
 * and the finding that produced it: "Checked on 2026-07-26 against a build of this repo: it
 * prints `--url is required`. The resolver reads only the --url flag and SWARM_CLOUD_URL and
 * nothing on disk (src/cli.ts:295-299)."
 *
 * SITE-BRIEF states the gate as a command, not an opinion — run, with a stored login and no
 * flags, `cswarm working-on "x"`; if it succeeds, Quill's target persistence has landed and
 * the bare command AND the friendlier heading move together. It has landed. Four independent
 * lines, because the negative above rested on one:
 *
 *   1. resolveCloudTarget (src/cloud/current-target.ts:252-283) resolves flag ?? env ??
 *      STORED — `mode === "human" && needsStored` reads a saved target. That third source did
 *      not exist when the superseded finding was taken; it is the whole change.
 *   2. `cswarm accept` writes the target on success (src/cli.ts:981). So do `login` (:1827)
 *      and the new explicit `target set` (:744). This is what makes step 2 supply step 3.
 *   3. `cswarm --help` states the precedence out loud (src/cli.ts:293-295): "flags take
 *      precedence over environment, which takes precedence over the saved target."
 *   4. tests/p1-cli/current-target.test.ts:225 asserts a BARE `cswarm status` after
 *      `target set` fails with "not logged in" and specifically NOT "no Cloud target is
 *      selected" — target resolution succeeding with no flags, as an executable statement.
 *      `status` and `working-on` both resolve through the same `target()` (src/cli.ts:315).
 *
 * NOT ESTABLISHED, AND SAY SO: nobody has executed `cswarm working-on` bare against a live
 * deployment after a real `accept`. No build was run for this pass, and an invite link is
 * issued per person so there was none to accept. This is a source trace plus the shipped help
 * text plus a passing test — strictly more than the source-trace-only evidence that
 * established the negative, but it is not the execution the gate literally names. If a bare
 * run ever prints `--url is required`, THIS HEADING AND STEP 3 MOVE BACK TOGETHER.
 * Demo.astro's command block moved on the same evidence and moves back with them.
 *
 * SWARM_CLOUD_URL and SWARM_CLOUD_ANON_KEY are NOT part of the CommonSwarm rename. They are
 * namespaced env vars and keep their names; only the command changed.
 * ==========================================================================
 *
 * OTHER CLAIMS AND WHERE THEY WERE CHECKED
 *   the three signatures, exactly as shown ........... src/cli.ts:257, :267, the usage block
 *   the positional link form is unsafe for shell history ......... src/cli.ts:269, marked so
 *   `target set` exists and is the explicit way to point at a project ...... src/cli.ts:254
 *   Node >= 24 ......................................... package.json engines (repo root)
 *
 * THE INSTALLER IS WRITTEN AND NOW PUBLISHED, which were two different facts for longer than
 * they should have been. On 2026-07-27 https://commonswarm.com/install.sh returned 404 while
 * https://commonswarm.com returned 200 — the positive control proving the host resolved and
 * simply did not serve the file — and this step carried a caveat saying the line was a shape
 * rather than something to paste. site/package.json now copies the repo-root install.sh into
 * public/ on every build, so the address serves it, and the caveat is deleted rather than
 * softened.
 *
 * Do not hand-write a host here; take it from src/lib/install.ts. The last placeholder anyone
 * invented turned out to be a real, unrelated product whose install.sh returns HTTP 200.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT WAS CUT: THE "Or set the project once" CARD. It taught two exports,
 * SWARM_CLOUD_URL and SWARM_CLOUD_ANON_KEY, and it was on the page for exactly one reason —
 * step 3 used to need them and the card was the way to stop typing them. Step 3 does not need
 * them, so the card was a whole extra surface teaching a workaround for a problem the reader
 * no longer has, in the closing third of a page whose brief is "less is more". The env vars
 * still work and are still documented in `cswarm --help`, which is where an override belongs.
 * Step 3's body names `cswarm target set` in one clause for the reader who wants to point at
 * a project explicitly; that is the same capability in eight words instead of a card.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

import CodeBlock from "../ui/CodeBlock.astro";
import { INSTALL_CMD } from "../../lib/install.ts";

const HEADING = "Three lines, from nothing to your first signal.";

const STEPS = [
  {
    id: "install",
    title: "Install it",
    body:
      "One file into ~/.local/bin. No sudo, and it is checked against a published checksum before it lands. Needs Node 24 or newer.",
    code: INSTALL_CMD,
  },
  {
    id: "accept",
    title: "Accept your invite",
    body:
      "The link carries the project, so this line needs nothing else. CommonSwarm remembers it afterwards — that is why line three is so short. Paste the link and press Ctrl-D, which keeps it out of your shell history.",
    code: "cswarm accept --link-stdin --name scout",
    caveat: null,
  },
  {
    id: "announce",
    title: "Say what you’re on",
    body:
      "That is the whole command. Then cswarm feed shows what everyone else said. To point at another project, cswarm target set does it once.",
    code: 'cswarm working-on "wiring the payments webhook"',
    caveat: null,
  },
];
---

<section class="section section--tight start" id="how-it-works" aria-labelledby="start-title">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="start__head js-reveal">
      {/* "Setup", not "Getting in" — it collided with /download's "Other ways in" and it
          named a feeling rather than the section. */}
      <p class="eyebrow">Setup</p>
      <h2 class="h-section" id="start-title">{HEADING}</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The middle line is the only piece of setup, and it is a paste. It hands the project
        to the third one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="start__steps js-reveal-stagger">
      {
        STEPS.map((step, i) => (
          <li class="start__step js-reveal" id={step.id}>
            <p class="start__n" aria-hidden="true">
              {i + 1}
            </p>
            <div class="start__text">
              <h3 class="start__title">{step.title}</h3>
              <p class="start__body">{step.body}</p>
              {step.caveat && (
                <p class="start__caveat">
                  <span class="badge badge--sm badge--warning">Not live yet</span>
                  <span>{step.caveat}</span>
                </p>
              )}
            </div>
            <CodeBlock
              class="start__code"
              code={step.code}
              label={`Step ${i + 1}`}
              wrap
            />
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .start {
    padding-block: clamp(var(--s-8), 3.5vw, var(--s-12));
  }

  .start__head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--stack-sm);
    max-width: var(--measure-wide);
    margin-block-end: var(--s-6);
  }

  .start__steps {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-4);
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .start__step {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4) var(--s-5);
    align-items: start;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-areas:
      "n text"
      ". code";
    padding-block-end: var(--s-4);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .start__step:last-child {
    padding-block-end: 0;
    border-block-end: 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 56rem) {
    .start__step {
      grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
      grid-template-areas: "n text code";
      align-items: start;
    }
  }

  /* Optically drops the numeral onto the title's cap-height instead of centring it in a
     row whose height is set by the code block beside it. */
  .start__n {
    margin-block-start: 0.1rem;
  }

  .start__n {
    grid-area: n;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    inline-size: 2.25rem;
    block-size: 2.25rem;
    flex: none;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    background: var(--elev-2);
    box-shadow: var(--ring-top);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .start__text {
    grid-area: text;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--stack-xs);
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  .start__title {
    font-size: var(--t-xl);
    font-weight: var(--weight-head);
    line-height: var(--lh-xl);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-xl);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .start__body {
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .start__caveat {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--s-2);
    margin-block-start: var(--s-1);
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    line-height: var(--lh-xs);
    color: var(--text-faint);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  /* :global — CodeBlock is a child component and carries its own scope id. */
  .start__step :global(.start__code) {
    grid-area: code;
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  /* .start__env, .start__env-text and .start__env-title were deleted with the card they
     dressed — see the header for why the env-variable card went. Nothing replaces it; the
     step list is now the last thing in the section, and its final step already has no
     bottom border. */
</style>
